After reading some other YAFAP I don't know how you guys remember what all
happened in such detail.  Let's see if I can come up with anything
interesting:

I played a bit of Nethack around 5 years ago or so, got distracted from
the game, stopped playing, and then a buddy of mine reminded about it so
I started up again about a month ago.  Oops.  Pretty much played the game
in all my free time for a couple of weeks.

I've only played wizards for the most part.  I start any wizard out and
see how far he got.  Usually something will kill him off pretty quickly.
The hapless wizards before this one died to:

  Fell through a trapdoor 4 levels and couldn't get away from
    monsters back to safety.

  Prayed on a converted altar and then ate a fresh corpse that
    somehow got me sick.

  Died to a swarm of killer bees that literally appeared around
    me (random monsters).

  Died to killer bees in sokoban because I couldn't cast my only
    offensive spell (force bolt) in any particular direction because it
    destroys boulders.

  Died to a rock landing on my head.

  Got stuck in a beartrap and got pounded on by an owlbear and a
    dwarf.

  Somehow got caught in a swarm of 10+ hill orcs that streamed out
    of a room and into both sides of a corridor.  They quickly killed
    my pet and then I knew it was over for me.

  Then three in a row died to soldier ants.


But this one made it.


I had some problems with my pets this game:

   Tony the Cat #1 - I was forced to kill with a reflecting magic 
                     missile, right after it had cleaned out an entire
                     store for me, due to being in a hallway with some
                     dangerous monsters.

   Tony the Cat #2 - I found and tamed a bones-file Tony the Cat 
                     and pretended he was the one I started with.  I 
                     then killed him by zapping a wand of polymorph 
                     at him.

   Unnamed Dog/Master Mind Flayer - 
     I found a polymorph trap in the mines and looked for a pet to tame
     with a tripe ration; I put it on a leash and dragged it down there.
     After a while it turned into a Master Mind Flayer.  The Mind Flayer
     was cool.  He kept sending me good vibes, then he killed himself
     on a blue jelly.  Not the first pet I've lost to a blue jelly, but I
     guess I just thought with all the brains they eat ...

   Warhorse - Some warhorse was kicking my butt while I was low on 
              hit points.  I threw an apple at it to calm it down 
              and it became my new pet.  It eventually died in combat.

   Unnamed Dog/Arch-lich -
     I found a polymorph trap in the main branch and found a pet and 
     polymorphed it.  It wound up as an arch-lich.  The arch-lich
     is pretty good for taking out shopkeepers but has an annoying habit
     of turning on you.  I was about 15th level at this point and had
     eaten a tengu corpse because I thought I had a ring of teleport 
     control.  The fact that the arch-lich teleports around with my jumpy
     self made for a pretty good pet.  Of course, he ended up turning
     on my as my pet arch-liches seem to like to, when I ran him over 
     while he was stuck in a cobweb.  Since the arch-liches always like to
     teleport right in front of me while I'm walking around I have a
     tendency to run into them if they get stuck in pits or webs, or
     whatever.  I had to kill him when he went wild.


No more pets after that.  I did accidentally sacrifice that warhorse,
though.  I was on a level with an altar and I was sacrificing everything
and praying, hoping for a few choice spellbooks or something, and there
were a couple of warhorse corpses.  I sacrificed the wrong one and ticked
my god off.

That took a while to take care of, being chaotic and all.  I ended up
killing off Juiblex and Yeenoghu while trying to mollify my god.  So that
took care of them early.

At about that point, though I was set.  I already had finger of death so
the quest nemesis was cake.  It seems that after you get the Eye, and as
long as you have magic missile, magic mapping, finger of death, and some
others (fireball, identify, light, jumping, create monster) you're pretty
unstoppable.

I hung out near an altar near Medusa's level and cast create monster and
sacrificed until I got silver dragon scales, magic mapping, and some other
goodies.  With the Eye you practically can't cast create monster fast 
enough to run out of spell points.

On Medusa's level some friendly gremlin was jumping in and out of the water
and I couldn't even get around them.  I ended up killing them all just to 
get clear passage and headed back up to sacrifice some more to pay 
penance for such a slaughter of friendlies (25+ gremlins at least).

I transported all of my stash down to Medusa's level.  Left it there
unprotected in a bag of holding, put on my ring of levitation, cruised
over, did my business, came back to my stash, and found a gelatinous cube
happily munching on ALL my stuff.  Uh.

So that was a pain.  I knew I was close to the castle, though, so I'd just
wish for another bag.

After that it was all just spellbook/weight management and whatnot.  I used
Magicbane the whole way through.  I had some fun with the stethoscope.  I
found that my magic missiles were doing something around 25-30 points of
damage to the demons while Magicbane was around 10 or so.

Finger of death made Rodney easy.  Priest of Moloch was easy.  Coming back
up was annoying due to getting thrown back down.  I got pushed back around
10 times or so.  Pretty lame, but more just annoying than anything.

I wrote some gold detection scrolls before climbing up out of level 1.
Earth was super easy.  Fire was easy.  Air was no big deal with a ring of
conflict.  Water was annoying and stressful due to the drowning monsters.

Astral was a bit confusing.  I wasn't too sure what to do so I ended up
fighting a few more monsters than I would have preferred.  I put the ring
of conflict on sometime during that level, though, and it helped out
significantly.

So that's that.  I'm pretty stoked.  Thanks to steelypips.org and Maniac's
Ascension Guide for greasing the wheels of this first ascension.


